s uh strategy for this autonomy in  March in Versailles there are three  pillars which are absolutely crucial in  which I fully support the first pillow  pillar is a energy sovereignty we're not  discussing this here because that is not  a topic of the day but it's something  that we work on in the Commission in the  council in order to have  a real uh Market single Market here and  have more Independence and sovereignty  regarding this matter the second pillar  is that of defense and security and this  is where the Strategic concept NATO's  strategic concept  complements supports and is consistent  with another strategic document which is  the EU document the Strategic compass  this does demonstrate that this topic is  extremely important to us  and with this in mind I would add that  let us not forget that the overnight  between the 23rd and the 24th of  February when President zielinski called  us a number of us were there and at EU  was able to very rapidly activate its  peace facility in order to provide  weapons to a country to Ukraine and I  think that this is  in a way that the birth of a European  Defense in a very effective Manner and  then there's also technological  sovereignty there's a more of an  economic approach which we did not  discuss today so just to confirm that  this whole notion of autonomy of  Independence is a very important in  order to have more resilience going  forward and it is quite clear that this  is something which needs to be done in  complementarity with our friends  partners and allies and we do this with  NATO and I said this at the time and I  would repeat that today  strong allies create solid alliances and  that is what we're doing today we are  reinforcing the EU we're reinforcing  NATO and this reinforces our unity in  the face of a threat which we are  dealing with because of this war which  was started by Russia  and the membership of NATO in NATO  I'm confident that the accession process  will be finalized and that uh and that  all NATO analyzers will ratify the  accession protocols in the parliaments  and and that also goes for turkey  but let me start by just reminding you  all about what has happened so far  this has been so far the quickest  accession process in NATO's modern  history we have to remember that  normally accession processes into NATO  takes years  uh Finland Sweden applied in May last  year already in July all 30 allies  including turkey  decided to invite Finance Sweden to  become full members of NATO and all 30  allies also signed the accession  protocols and so far 20 28 out of 30  allies have already ratified this is  quicker than ever before in NATO's  modern history  um then uh what paved the way for this  uh historic decision was the agreement  between turkey Finland and Sweden that  was also assigned on the margins of the  NATO Summit in Madrid in July where they  agreed to work more closer together  Finance with agrees to lift the  restrictions on arms exports that has  already been done and they also agreed  to work more closely in the fight  against terrorism that that is also  taking place they have established a  permanent mechanism where they exchange  more information intelligence and and  they are working more closely in in how  to address terrorist threats turkey has  legitimate security concerns no NATO  Ally has suffered more terrorist attacks  than a turkey and therefore it is  something which is welcomed that the  NATO allies Finland Sweden are working  can work closely on these these issues  and then let me add one more thing and  that is that we have to understand that  Finland and Sweden they are in a very  different place now uh compared to where  they were before they applied for a NATO  membership since they applied several  NATO allies including the United States  have agreed a bilateral security  Arrangements security assurances for  Finland and Sweden NATO has increased  its presence in the region and as  invitees Finland and Sweden are now  participating in NATO's meetings  ministerial meetings ambassadora  meetings meetings of different NATO  committees and um and and finance  Vietnam War and more integrated into  NATO's military structures  so it's inconceivable that Finland and  Sweden will face any military threat  without NATO reacting to that so I'm  saying that I I of course see the  importance of finalizing the accession  process with the ratification in both  the Hungarian and the Turkish Parliament  but I'm also saying that I'm confident  that will happen and that we have  already moved a very long way in a short  time with Finland and Sweden  then I've had a few words on the topic  of strategic autonomy a lot has happened  in the last year  and president Michelle is right when he  said that at the beginning of the year  because strategic autonomy is not only  limited to defense it includes of course  defense  and strategic autonomy does not say that  you do not cooperate you cooperate with  like-minded partners  and the fields in which we have made a  lot of progress the first one is very  well known and this is the vaccine  production so I don't have to explain  that but a big step forward on that  we've done during the pandemic  the second field indeed is or was our  toxic dependency on Russian fossil fuels  We have basically completely gotten rid  of it during this year coal completely  we have wind down a Russian oil and more  than 80 percent of the Russian gas has  been cut we have been able and that  strategic autonomy to diversify  our supply chains to trusted and  like-minded partners  and of course to ramp up the own  production of energy we have doubled the  deployment of additional renewable  energy in the European Union and this is  Independence renewable energy is not  only clean and affordable but  Independence  then we have been working because we saw  a growing dependency on semiconductors  on the chips act also a success story  we are preparing the raw materials act  because we see the same  um challenge what the supply of raw  material is concerned too much focused  on one region of the world  and we have developed global gateway  also to build up these Partnerships with  like-minded on the whole energy topic  for example with the global South so a  lot has happened in the topic of  strategic autonomy final Point defense  we are working now with the member  states through a task force jointly with  the eeas and the European Defense Agency  to figure out what are the needs for the  replenishment of the stocks and what  does the industry need to cater for it  because it takes two here not only the  demand but also of course the supply  side and this is another step forward in  organizing and harmonizing a European  common defense answer  okay we'll go to the Ukrainian news  agency Ukraine form  thank you foreign  I want to mention that ukrainians are  grateful uh very much to both  organization both to EU and NATO for  practical solidarity my question is that  the both organizations now have the  common problem of depletion of military  stocks because nobody expected the war  in Ukraine of such a scale my question  is what is the Practical cooperation  between NATO and EU in filling that Gap  and the second part of the question as  the Russian now mobilized reservists and  preparing some kind of a future Advance  will EU and NATO be able to be in time  and prepared to assist Ukraine in these  battle which could be decisive thank you  maybe I could start by saying that you  are right that NATO allies and EU  members have depleted their stocks to  provide support to Ukraine and that has  been the right thing to do because this  is also about our security and of course  we need to use our uh  capabilities our stocks our ammunition  to support Ukraine and I've also been  asked and I've told NATO allies that if  they had to choose between meeting all  the NATO guidelines on stocks on  capabilities or to support Ukraine it's  more important to support Ukraine uh and  um and and therefore we have been able  to provide a unprecedented level of  support to Ukraine over many months but  of course uh when we are depleting our  stocks there is only one long-term uh  solution to that and that is to produce  more uh and uh that's also reason why  NATO defense ministers took decisions to  increase our stockpiles of Munitions and  Equipment when they met  last fall we have also a long-standing  defense planning process and capability  targets for each and every Ally  including stocks and ammunition and we  are revising them to ensure that we have  enough stocks both to protect our own  territory but also to provide support  for Ukraine and we are also working with  the European Union we had a commissioner  here just before Christmas and and we  are on the staff level looking into how  we can coordinate our efforts and  because this is about ramping of  production of course in EU NATO allies  but also in allies that are not the  members of the European Union and we  have engaged directly within the state  and they are now ramping up production  the aim is of course to ensure that we  can continue and also strengthen our  support to Ukraine because we have seen  that Russia has  suffered uh uh big losses in in Ukraine  due to the brave Ukrainian soldiers  under and the professionalism and  education of the Ukrainian Armed Forces  but we should not underestimate Russia  they are mobilizing more troops they are  working hard to acquire more equipment  more ammunition and they are shown a  willingness to actually suffer but  continue the war and there is no  indication that President Putin has  changed the overall aim of his uh brutal  war against Ukraine so we need to be  prepared for the Long Haul we need to  continue to support Ukraine and again it  is a a extremely important message that  we're sending today with the Declaration  with the two presidents hair present  with me that we stand United  NATO and the European Union in our  support to Ukraine  we're running out of time but we'll take  one third question which is a DPA  thank you how's the German press agency  DPA a great question to all of you on  the ongoing discussions on weapon  deliveries to Ukraine do you support  member states that are considering  sending Western main battle tanks like  the Leo part 2 or the Challenger 2 to  Ukraine or do you feel that there's a  huge risk of escalation thank you  I have said many times since the  beginning of Russia's invasion in  Ukraine that I think that Ukraine should  God get all the necessary military  equipment they need and they can handle  to defend their Homeland and this means  of course Advanced Air Defense systems  but also other types of advanced  military equipment as long as it is  necessary to defend Ukraine because they  also defend the basic principles of the  U.N Charter of the fundamental rights  and of the international law and I've  said that at the very beginning of  Russia's war and I have still this  position of course  let me just add that um  that what you have seen is that  we NATO allies EU members have uh  provided a Ukraine also with Advanced  weapon systems since the war started and  I welcome the new announcements uh just  lost the days by the United States  Germany France to also provide new types  of  um of armored vehicles  infantry fighting vehicles and other  types of armor to to Ukraine this is  important there is a constant  consultation within the alliance between  the NATO allies but also with Ukraine  we'll meet next week in Ramstein in the  U.S led contact group to provide support  to Ukraine and they will meet with the  Ukrainian defense minister to discuss  exactly what types of weapons are needed  and how can allies provide those weapons  so so in addition to armor we have also  provided Advanced Air Defense systems as  president Von Ryan Russell just refer to  and also long-range missiles with the  high Mars and and all these systems are  making a huge difference on the  battlefield every day  I welcome the focus on systems but I  think we need to also realize that this  is not only about adding more systems  more platforms more weapons but also  ensuring that the platforms the weapons  we have already provided are working as  they should meaning that we also need to  ensure that we provide the necessary  ammunition the spare parts the training  the maintenance of the systems which are  already provided and therefore yes it is  important to address what new systems do  we need to provide to Ukraine but also  to ensure that all the systems which are  already there are working as they should  there is a there is an addresser there  is a Kremlin and there is a victim this  is the people of Ukraine and I'm very  pleased that uh in the in the recent  weeks and months following the start of  the war the the member states were able  to deliver more and more military  equipment to to Ukraine and uh like  osula said I fully support the same  approach and the European Council many  times repeated the the call on more  military support for Ukraine or to make  sure that they will be able to to defend  their side because we know that the  fighting for the future but they are  also fighting for our common values  thank you very much I'm sorry we can't  take all the questions we have but this  concludes this press conference thank  you  [Music]
 

